    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d310">

                <pb facs="0385" n="292" sameAs="frus:frus1935v04/pg292"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.51B861/140</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Executive Vice President and Secretary of the
                        Foreign Bondholders Protective Council, Inc.</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">White</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Chief of the Division of Latin American Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>)<anchor n="27"
                        xml:id="d310-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New
                                York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1935-10-02"
                            >October 2, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received October 9.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Wilson</hi>: Please accept my hearty thanks
                    for your letter of the 1st inst. (LA 621.6317/35),<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d310-f2"/> enclosing a paraphrase of telegram No. 220, September
                    27th, 2 p.m., from the Embassy at Buenos Aires. <pb facs="0386" n="293"
                        xml:id="pg293"/> I am indeed happy to have this information, which comes at
                    a most opportune moment when we are considering a new proposal of the Province
                    of Buenos Aires.</p>
                <p>Yesterday, they suggested paying, on the 6%, 6½%, 7% and 7%% Bonds respectively,
                    as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>1936–1938—4⅛—4¼—4⅜—4½—no amortization.</p>
                        <p>1939–1941— ditto —¾% amortization.</p>
                        <p>1942–until bonds are retired—4⅜—4½—4⅝—4¾—1% amortization.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>In considering this matter it would be a help to us to have an answer to our
                    cable of August 23rd to Señor Pinedo, Minister of Finance of the Republic. I am
                    wondering whether the Embassy is having any success in this matter?</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Francis White</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
